Task Prompt – High‑Level Overview

Objective:

Modernise the MythWorx OSINT framework so that it respects a user‑chosen privacy mode (public, private, hybrid), automatically updates every registered CLI‑tool repository after one week, and intelligently selects & installs the correct local binary (e.g., whois for domains, nmap for IPs) during the HARVESTING stage.

The system must also be able to switch between a local LLM (Ollama/LM‑Studio) or a cloud LLM based on the chosen privacy mode, and store all decisions in persistent JSON files (~/.osint_last_run.json, ~/.cli_tools.json).

All new behaviour should be added without breaking the existing pipeline; the agent will therefore:

add a --privacy flag to main.py;
propagate that flag through the pipeline configuration;
modify key‑validation logic accordingly;
create an LLM helper that returns either a local or cloud client;
implement a time‑based auto‑update routine for tool repos;
extend PipelineConfig with a tool_repos dictionary;
add a “target‑type → CLI tool” mapping inside the wrapper and a public method execute_tool_for_target;
trigger that method from the HARVESTING stage;
persist binary paths in ~/.cli_tools.json.
After the changes, running python main.py "example.com" should:

ask to install whois on first use (or skip if already installed),
launch it automatically during harvesting,
generate a report (PDF/HTML) with the chosen LLM, and
update any tool repos if more than 7 days have passed since the last run.
The following detailed step‑by‑step instructions will guide the agent through each of these changes.

[  What the agent must implement / verify  -	Where in the codebase (file + function) -	Expected behaviour ]

1	Add a --privacy command‑line flag (public, private, hybrid). The default (hybrid) should keep the current “optional key” logic. -	main.py  argparse section.	User can explicitly choose privacy mode.

2	Propagate the flag into the pipeline configuration so that every stage (RECON, HARVESTING, ANALYST) knows the chosen mode. -	OSINTPipeline.__init__, store as self.privacy_mode.	All API‑client builders read this value.

3	Modify key‑validation – a missing key is only an error when private; in public it is silently ignored; hybrid keeps the original “optional” behaviour.	_validate_api_keys() inside OSINTPipeline.	Running with --privacy public works even without keys.

4	Create a LLM‑selection helper – based on self.privacy_mode decide whether to use the local Ollama/LM‑Studio client or a cloud endpoint (e.g., OpenAI). This should be exposed as get_llm_client() that returns a callable client(prompt).	Add a new method in OSINTPipeline.	When privacy is public, the agent can call a fast cloud LLM; when private it must use the local client.

5	Persist the “last run” timestamp (already present as _load_last_run/_save_last_run). The pipeline should, before starting, compare current epoch with stored value and if > 7 days call a new helper that updates all registered tool repos.	OSINTPipeline.run_investigation() – add the time‑check and call await self._update_registered_tools(config).	After 7 days the system automatically pulls the latest commit of each repo in config.tool_repos.

6	Extend PipelineConfig to accept a tool_repos: Dict[str,str] (key = tool name, value = GitHub URL). Update its __init__ accordingly.	osint_kanban_manager.py – where PipelineConfig is instantiated.	The manager now knows which tools exist and can ask the wrapper to use them.

7	Add a “tool‑by‑target” mapping inside osint_cli_wrapper. Example: {"domain": ["whois"], "ip_address": ["nmap"]}. Create a method execute_tool_for_target(target_type, target_value) that looks up the binary (using the persisted map), prompts for installation if missing, clones via ToolProvisioner, records path in ~/.cli_tools.json and finally runs the command with a sane timeout.	osint_cli_wrapper.py.	During HARVESTING the wrapper will automatically launch the correct CLI tool.

8	Trigger the wrapper from the HARVESTING stage – after each API result is processed, inspect its type (e.g., if it contains an IP or a domain) and call cli_runner.execute_tool_for_target(...).	OSINTKanbanManager._process_harvesting().	CLI tools are now used explicitly in the pipeline.

9	Implement the auto‑update routine (_update_registered_tools) that iterates over config.tool_repos, creates a ToolProvisioner instance, calls provision_tool_from_github(..., security_check=False) and ignores errors (logging only).	Inside OSINTPipeline.	After 7 days the system stays up‑to‑date without user intervention.

10	Add a helper to choose between local LLM (Ollama/LM‑Studio) or cloud – based on privacy_mode and an optional env variable USE_CLOUD_LLM. Return a callable that accepts a prompt string and returns the completion text.	New method in OSINTPipeline.	The ANALYST stage can now ask for fact verification via the chosen LLM.

11	Update README / docs – note the new flags, privacy behaviour and how to add new tool repos (edit config.tool_repos).	Documentation files.	Users understand how to extend the system.


